easywall (2.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: connection_limit_max and eight other firewall limits had three
    different sets of bounds and none of them was enforced. A limit landing
    in a 32-bit nftables field did not fail when too large — it wrapped:
    4294967296 arrived as `ct count over 0`, a rule that drops every
    connection from every source, with nothing logged. One table now carries
    the range for all nine, and the daemon enforces it rather than the page
    or the schema hinting at it
  * Security: GET /logout sat outside Go's CrossOriginProtection, which
    exempts safe methods by design, so any page the operator had open could
    end their session with a single cross-site image tag. It is a POST now
  * Fix: an unattended package upgrade could leave easywall unconfigured,
    with the old processes still serving. easywall.toml was shipped as a
    dpkg conffile, and easywall rewrites it on every saved setting, which
    dpkg reads as locally modified — a release that also changed the
    shipped default stopped the upgrade at the conffile prompt and left it
    "install ok unpacked": new binaries on disk, postinst never run. It
    ships as easywall.toml.template now, the arrangement web.toml has
    always had
  * Fix: the post-incident nftables snapshot attributed chains to the wrong
    table when a same-named table existed in another family, and reported
    an unreadable rule count as zero rather than as unreadable
  * Fix: a mistyped or padded network in easywall.toml's Docker or routing
    lists reached the kernel as no rule at all, or as a rule in one chain
    and not its neighbour; the network editor, the core and the demo now
    share one definition of what a network is
  * Fix: an import that succeeded could be reported as failed under a slow
    nft --check; the client's deadline now matches the one the core itself
    enforces
  * Feat: --write-config, the flag the documentation had already promised,
    writes either binary's commented default configuration file
  * Feat: a Debian package for arm64, built and verified on a runner of its
    own architecture
  * Four documented pages the interface already had: First Run, Applying
    Rules, Dashboard, and a Roadmap page
  * Diagram rendering, CI guards and further documentation corrections —
    see CHANGELOG.md

